import{_ as t,o,c as s,ag as d}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"The srt settings of the CLI frontends","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"components/sandbox.md","filePath":"components/sandbox.md"}'),n={name:"components/sandbox.md"};function a(l,e,r,i,c,h){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d(`<h1 id="the-srt-settings-of-the-cli-frontends" tabindex="-1">The srt settings of the CLI frontends <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-srt-settings-of-the-cli-frontends" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The srt settings of the CLI frontends&quot;">​</a></h1><p><code>srt</code>, the sandbox runtime, runs one process and everything that it starts inside one sandbox. It reads one settings file. <code>harness install</code> writes one such file for each CLI frontend that the profile&#39;s <code>[setup]</code> chooses:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/claude.json</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/opencode.json</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/omp.json</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/antigravity.json</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Nothing runs under <code>srt</code> yet. A launcher that starts a frontend under <code>srt</code> with its file is a later change; until then the files have no effect.</p><p>The files come from two sources. The <code>sandbox</code> section of the Claude Code settings template, <code>settings/settings.proposal.json</code>, stays the source of the lists that every frontend shares: the write paths, the write and read denies, the credential files and the network rules, with the domains that <code>settings/network.toml</code> and the profile&#39;s overlay give. The policy file <code>sandbox/policy.toml</code> holds only what <code>srt</code> adds to them. <code>lib/harness/sandbox.py</code> renders the files from both.</p><h2 id="the-policy-file" tabindex="-1">The policy file <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-policy-file" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The policy file&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>sandbox/policy.toml</code> has a table <code>[read]</code> and a table <code>[frontend.&lt;name&gt;]</code> for each of <code>claude</code>, <code>opencode</code>, <code>omp</code> and <code>antigravity</code>. Every path starts with <code>~/</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>read.allow</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the paths that every frontend reads under the read limit</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.state</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the frontend&#39;s own directories and files, which it reads and writes</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.deny_write</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">write denies that the frontend adds to the template&#39;s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.own</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s deny entries that the frontend reads and writes itself</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The read limit denies reads of the whole home directory and allows the paths of <code>read.allow</code> again. A path that the template denies stays hidden inside an allowed path: in <code>srt</code> a deny inside an allowed path wins. Claude Code&#39;s <code>own</code> is <code>~/.claude.json</code>, which Claude Code writes itself; a write deny of it would stop the whole process under <code>srt</code>. The cost is that a command that Claude Code runs can also write the file, because the sandbox of the whole process cannot tell the frontend from its own shell. oh-my-pi adds the write denies <code>~/.omp/agent/config.yml</code> and <code>~/.omp/agent/extensions</code>.</p><p><code>harness install</code> exits with status 2 on a policy file with an unknown key, a missing key or frontend, a value that is not a list of strings, a path that does not start with <code>~/</code>, or a path with a <code>..</code> segment, which could leave the home directory. The message names the key or the path.</p><h2 id="the-rendering-rules" tabindex="-1">The rendering rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rendering-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rendering rules&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>srt_settings</code> gives one frontend&#39;s object, with the keys <code>network</code> and <code>filesystem</code> and no other:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>network.allowedDomains</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, in order</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>network.deniedDomains</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, or <code>[]</code> where it has none; <code>srt</code> refuses a file without the key</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>network.allowLocalBinding</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.allowWrite</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, without each entry at or below another frontend&#39;s <code>state</code> path; then the frontend&#39;s <code>state</code>; then the real path of <code>$TMPDIR</code>, where it is set and not empty</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.denyWrite</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, without the frontend&#39;s <code>own</code>; then its <code>deny_write</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.denyRead</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the home directory; then the template&#39;s; then each path of <code>credentials.files</code>; without the frontend&#39;s <code>own</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.allowRead</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>read.allow</code>; then the frontend&#39;s <code>state</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each <code>~</code> and <code>~/</code> prefix becomes the home directory, so the files hold absolute paths. A list holds no entry twice, and the first occurrence keeps its place. &quot;At or below&quot; compares whole path components: <code>~/.claude.json</code> is not below <code>~/.claude</code>. So no frontend&#39;s file allows a write to another frontend&#39;s state. A glob of the template, such as <code>~/Research/**/.git/hooks</code>, passes through after the expansion. The template&#39;s <code>excludedCommands</code> is not carried: under <code>srt</code> the whole process is inside the sandbox, so no command can run outside it. The masked credentials of <code>srt</code> are not used.</p><h2 id="the-install" tabindex="-1">The install <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-install" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The install&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>harness install</code> renders the Claude Code settings template as it does for <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>, with the profile and the network overlay, and gives its <code>sandbox</code> object to <code>srt_settings</code>. It writes each file as JSON indented by two spaces, with the keys in the order above and a final newline. The files belong to no frontend&#39;s plan: a change of them prints no restart line. A frontend that <code>[setup]</code> does not choose gets no file, and with no frontend chosen the install renders no file. The install does not change what it writes to <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>. Because it renders the template with the network overlay for every chosen frontend, a broken overlay stops the install with status 2 also when <code>claude</code> is not chosen, for example an <code>allow</code> entry that is a URL.</p>`,16)])])}const y=t(n,[["render",a]]);export{p as __pageData,y as default};
